\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 This significantly changes the SSO strategy.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Why this changes things:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. Heterogeneous Stack: You have a FastAPI-based newboard (Python) and a\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 PHP-based online-exam.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. Authentication Mismatch: The JWT logic in Python/FastAPI will not work\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 directly with a standard PHP session-based authentication without some\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 "bridge" logic.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 How to achieve SSO with PHP & FastAPI:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 To make these work together, you cannot just share a JWT secret. Instead, you\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 need a Shared Session or OpenID Connect (OIDC) approach:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Option A: Shared Cookie Store (Redis): Both the PHP app and the FastAPI app\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 could use Redis to store session data. The LMS portal (your entry point) would\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 create a session in Redis, and both the PHP and FastAPI apps would check that\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 same Redis store to authenticate the user.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Option B: OIDC/OAuth2 (Recommended): Use the LMS portal as an OIDC provider.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 When a user navigates to the PHP app or the FastAPI app, if they aren't logged\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 in, they are redirected to the LMS portal to authenticate. The portal returns\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 a code that the apps exchange for a local session.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Before we proceed, could you clarify:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Does the PHP online-exam application currently use a standard session-based\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 login (i.e., $_SESSION in PHP)?\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Are you open to using a standard authentication protocol like OIDC to link\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 these different technologies together?\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
