  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Strategy for SSO:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Because the PHP app relies on these custom sessions, the most reliable way to\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 integrate it with your FastAPI/Next.js ecosystem is to Externalize the\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Authentication.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. Stop PHP from handling logins directly: Modify the PHP app's login controller\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 so it doesn't just rely on its own database, but checks against the same\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 central user store (or calls an Auth API).\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. Shared Session Database (Redis/MySQL):\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Since the PHP app is already talking to MySQL, the easiest path is for\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 your FastAPI apps to share the same MySQL user table that the PHP app\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 uses.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * FastAPI will authenticate the user, create a session record in the same\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 MySQL table/schema, and then set a cookie that the PHP Session class can\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 recognize (you might need to adapt the PHP Session class to read from the\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 session table in the database).\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
