\f2\ltrch\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\u9600?\f1\lang1033\par
\f2\u10022?\f1  That is a great question. In a professional architecture, you should only have\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 one single source of truth for authentication.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 If you implement the centralized approach, here is how it works:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. The Goal: "Centralized-Only"\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 You should not have both methods running simultaneously in the long run. If you\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 keep both:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Security Risk: If a user account is deleted in the central store but still\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 exists in the local PHP database, that user might still gain access.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Data Inconsistency: You will constantly struggle to keep passwords or user\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 roles in sync between two places.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. How the transition works (The "Bridge" Phase)\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 You don't have to "kill" the local authentication instantly. You can follow a\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Phased Transition:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Phase 1 (The Bridge): You modify the PHP login controller. When a user tries\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 to login:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. The PHP app first checks if the user exists in the Central Store (e.g.,\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 via an API call to your FastAPI auth service).\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. If the credentials are valid, the PHP app automatically "imports" or\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 "syncs" that user into its own local database (if they aren't there\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 already).\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 3. The PHP app then creates its local session.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Result: The user feels like they logged in "locally," but you are actually\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
         \f1\ltrch\lang1033 verifying them against the central system.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Phase 2 (True SSO): Once all your users are in the central system, you remove\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 the PHP local password verification logic entirely. The PHP application simply\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 trusts a token or a session created by the Central Login Portal.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 3. Will the PHP app be "Self-Authenticated"?\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 In this model, no. The PHP app becomes a "Service Provider." It says: "I don't\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 know who this user is, but the Central Portal told me they are User X, so I trust\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 the portal."\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 My Recommendation\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Since you have a new lms-platform-frontend (your entry point) and FastAPI\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 backends, I suggest:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. Use your LMS Entry Portal to manage the login.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. FastAPI Backends validate a shared JWT (the modern, standard way).\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 3. PHP Online-Exam remains as a "Legacy Service Provider." You don't need to\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 rewrite it; you just need to add a "Login Middleware" to it that accepts an\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
      \f1\ltrch\lang1033 authentication token from the LMS portal and converts it into a PHP session.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
