\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Here is the good news: You can preserve the existing internal functions while\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 changing only the login gate.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 The "Session Emulation" Strategy\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 The key to not breaking your internal functions is to make sure that after the\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 user successfully logs in, the PHP session still looks exactly the way it did\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 before.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. How it works\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Your PHP app currently relies on the Session object. When a user logs in\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 manually, the app does something like this:\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1 // Existing internal logic (which you want to keep)\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2 Session::set("user_id", $user->id);\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 3 Session::set("role", $user->role);\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 As long as these sessions exist, your internal functions (like\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 get_user_courses()) will continue to work perfectly because they don't care how\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 the session was created\f4\lang1025\emdash\f1\lang1033 they only care that it exists.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. The Bridge Logic\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Instead of removing your current login code, we will intercept it.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * Before: User types username/password -> PHP code validates -> PHP code creates\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Session.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * After (The Change):\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 1. The user logs in via your Central LMS Portal.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 2. The Central Portal redirects the user to the PHP app with a "Login\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Token."\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 3. The PHP app receives the token, calls your FastAPI Auth API to verify it,\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 and receives the User Data back.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 4. Crucially: The PHP app then takes that user data and populates the\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Session object exactly as it used to do.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
       \f1\ltrch\lang1033 5. Your internal functions continue to run as if nothing happened because\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
          \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Session::get("user_id") is still returning the correct value.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
  \f1\ltrch\lang1033 Will this affect your internal functions?\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * If your internal functions rely on standard $_SESSION or Session::get(): No,\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 they will not be affected.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
   \f1\ltrch\lang1033 * If your internal functions check a database column that won't exist in the new\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 system: Possibly. We will need to ensure that the "user syncing" process I\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 mentioned (where the PHP app creates a local user record on the first login)\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 is working, so the database rows your internal functions expect are always\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
     \f1\ltrch\lang1033 there.\f0\rtlch\lang1025\par
\par
